{"video_id": "DKzaXN3wt_I", "source_file": "Everyone Talks About Interest Rates. Nobody Explains What_s Coming. [DKzaXN3wt_I].mp4", "source_sha256": "aed8d72fdc9ead76906c91792da154a1dacc9e07fa664d9e2d7b469b548823fb", "local_dur": 61.28, "model": "base", "engine": "openai-whisper", "status": "ASR_UNCERTIFIED", "device": "cpu", "elapsed_s": 4.6, "language": "en", "text": " And when rates rise this aggressively, there's consequences. In early 2022, sofa was effectively zero. Then the Fed started hiking, and by mid-2023, sofa was over 5%. And now, even after a year and a half of rate cuts, it's still sitting higher than where we were before the pandemic. Because when most people think about debt, they think fixed rate. Like a mortgage. You lock in an interest rate on day one. The rate environment can do whatever it wants afterward, and your payment doesn't change. But there's an entire other category of debt called floating rate, which works the opposite way. A floating rate loan doesn't pick a fixed rate at origination. It picks a benchmark, which is almost always sofa, and then it adds a spread on top. That spread is the lender's premium for taking on the risk. With floating rate loans, the interest rate on the loan is directly tied to the benchmark it's pegged to. So if sofa moves, the interest rate on the loan moves too. Meaning if sofa rises, the borrower's interest on the debt rises. This is where the consequences of rapidly rising rates begin to show up.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.1, "text": " And when rates rise this aggressively, there's consequences.", "tokens": [50364, 400, 562, 6846, 6272, 341, 32024, 11, 456, 311, 10098, 13, 50532], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12832879638671876, "compression_ratio": 1.561128526645768, "no_speech_prob": 0.05171068385243416, "words": [{"word": " And", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.22, "probability": 0.637884259223938}, {"word": " when", "start": 0.22, "end": 0.34, "probability": 0.9581753611